utside of the  Amazon web services company and more  broadly how to find out which tech  companies are actually hiring before we  get into how to find those jobs I like  to mention that this video is in  collaboration with school up now more  than ever it's important for you to  continue building your skills to stand  out in the job market screw up is a  learning platform by simply learn and  then now offer over 200 free courses to  help you gain in-demand technical skills  which could help you get high paying  jobs the courses are self-paced and  available from both your browser and  their mobile app meaning you can learn  anywhere at any time you want the video  lessons are prepared by industry experts  and aims to help fast track your  learning as you can see here they have a  few introduction to AWS courses that are  completely free of cost the best part is  you can complete the courses in just a  few hours and what's pretty cool is at  the end of each course you'll be awarded  with a certificate of completion these  certificates can then help validate your  skill set in AWS and other Technologies  so to start learning with screw up check  out the link in the video description  below that directs to the sign up page  now let's get back to the video okay so  how do you find AWS jobs that are  outside of the AWS company let me  introduce you to Sally Sally has just  passed the AWS Solutions Arctic  associate exam last month and has built  two AWS Cloud projects she's now ready  to start applying to jobs and her dream  job is to either become an AWS Cloud  engineer or any previous devops engineer  but she finds out that Amazon has just  announced a hiring phrase and thinks to  herself is a game over then do I still  have a chance of Landing a job in the  cloud here's what I'd recommend to Sally  first head to  partners.amazonaws.com then enter your  location for example Australia here you  can see a whole list of companies in  your country that AWS has partnered up  with to help customers build Cloud  Solutions on AWS not all of these  companies might be hiring but I  recommend taking a look at the job  portals to see if there's a role you  might be interested in if Sally finds  this too time consuming and doesn't want  to look at each job portal one by one  she can go onto a job search website  like indeed by searching AWS associate  she'll be able to find associate level  jobs that are looking for people with  AWS Cloud skills for example let's take  a look at this associate devops engineer  role they are looking to hire someone  with a practitioner level AWS or Azure  certification and or a degree with a  focus on software engineering slash  development Sally has two AWS  certifications so this shouldn't be an  issue they they mentioned they don't  need an expert but they want someone  interested in Cloud infrastructure and  has a fundamental knowledge of public  Cloud Concepts and also basic  programming and scripting experience and  that's a great example of how to find an  AWS job that's outside of the AWS  company with the Amazon hiring freeze  going on roles in the company will be  limited so this strategy will really  help you find jobs that are outside of  AWS where you still get to use your  Cloud skills for those watching who are  open to exploring positions outside of  cloud computing there's actually a  website that lists out the companies  still looking to hire people if you head  to levels.fyi forward slash still hiring  you'll see a list of companies and the  types of jobs they're hiring for you can  filter by role for example if you select  devops you can see which companies are  looking for devops engineers it seems  like there's a bunch of remote positions  available as well so if you want the  flexibility to work from home take a  look at the companies that offer remote  work one bonus tip is to make sure you  turn your resume to each position you  apply for I know at this point it might  be tempting to just submit your resume  to to every single job opening you see  but because there are less open roles  available it's important to make sure  you can stand out in the application  process this means making sure your  resume is up to date with your work  experience certifications education and  projects but also instead of just  dumping everything onto one or two pages  make sure you pick the relevant  experiences and skills that best align  with the job description if you're  someone looking to land a job in Tech  please leave a comment below and I'd  love to get back to you with some  tailored advice and as always please  give this video a like if you found it  helpful and I'll see you soon bye for  now
 

